></a></li><li><a href="/review-media/review/derived/aadhaar-pulse/xgboost_feature_importance.1280.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="View larger image: AadhaarPulse — project analysis chart (opens in a new tab)"><img src="/review-media/review/derived/aadhaar-pulse/xgboost_feature_importance.480.webp" width="480" height="237" alt="AadhaarPulse — project analysis chart" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></a></li></ul><h4>Problem</h4><p>Large authentication datasets can hide regional differences in demand and service quality.</p><h4>What it does</h4><p>The dashboard brings maps, district comparisons, demand indicators, and anomaly views into one interface. Python analysis supports the project; the web interface also contains static analytical data. It demonstrates an approach to governance analytics rather than an operational government monitoring service.</p><h4>Verified features</h4><ul class="bullets"><li>District and state comparisons</li><li>Geographic and demand visualizations</li><li>Anomaly and priority views</li></ul><h4>Architecture</h4><p>Next.js interface over analytical data exports and static frontend data; Python analysis is separate from the browser application.</p><p class="flag" data-review-mode>Needs owner review · media not yet cleared for release.</p></article><article class="record" id="record-ai-limit-checker" data-record-view="ai-limit-checker" hidden aria-labelledby="record-ai-limit-checker-title"><a class="back" href="/projects/" data-nav="projects"><svg class="icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M20.5 12h-16M10 6.5 4.5 12l5.5 5.5"/></svg> Projects</a><h3 class="record__title" id="record-ai-limit-checker-title" tabindex="-1">AI Limit Checker</h3><p class="record__kicker">Tools · Experiment · Prototype</p><p class="lead">A Chrome extension prototype for displaying token estimates and usage signals beside AI chats.</p><ul class="tags" role="list" aria-label="Technologies"><li>JavaScript</li><li>Chrome Extensions</li><li>DOM APIs</li></ul><div class="actions"><a class="btn" href="https://github.com/URAYUSHJAIN/Ai-limit-checker" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><svg class="icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M9 19.6c-4 1.2-4-2-5.6-2.4M14.6 21.6v-3.4c0-1 .1-1.4-.5-2 2.8-.3 5.6-1.4 5.6-6.2a4.8 4.8 0 0 0-1.3-3.3 4.5 4.5 0 0 0-.1-3.3s-1-.3-3.4 1.3a11.6 11.6 0 0 0-6 0C6.5 3.1 5.4 3.4 5.4 3.4a4.5 4.5 0 0 0-.1 3.3A4.8 4.8 0 0 0 4 10c0 4.8 2.8 5.9 5.6 6.2-.6.6-.6 1.2-.5 2v3.4"/></svg> View source on GitHub<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a></div><figure class="media"><video controls preload="none" playsinline src="/review-media/review/projects/ai-limit-checker/ai-limit-checker.mp4" aria-label="AI Limit Checker walkthrough video"></video><figcaption>Project walkthrough. No captions are available; the description below covers the same work.</figcaption></figure><h4>Problem</h4><p>Chat interfaces expose different levels of information about context windows and usage limits.</p><h4>What it does</h4><p>Provider-specific adapters collect available page and response information for a floating usage panel. The project combines token estimation, DOM observation, and response interception. Estimates depend on the provider and available signals; they are not authoritative billing or quota measurements.</p><h4>Verified features</h4><ul class="bullets"><li>Provider adapters</li><li>Floating usage panel</li><li>Token estimation</li></ul><h4>Architecture</h4><p>Chrome extension adapters → DOM/response signals → token-estimation core and floating interface.</p><p class="flag" data-review-mode>Needs owner review · some details conflict between sources and are withheld · media not yet cleared for release.</p></article><article class="record" id="record-arogya-plus" data-record-view="arogya-plus" hidden aria-labelledby="record-arogya-plus-title"><a class="back" href="/projects/" data-nav="projects"><svg class="icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M20.5 12h-16M10 6.5 4.5 12l5.5 5.5"/></svg> Projects</a><h3 class="record__title" id="record-arogya-plus-title" tabindex="-1">ArogyaPlus</h3><p class="record__kicker">Healthcare · Group project · Prototype</p><p class="lead">A healthcare interface prototype for uploading reports, reading AI explanations, and locating hospitals.</p><ul class="tags" role="list" aria-label="Technologies"><li>React</li><li>Vite</li><li>Tailwind CSS</li><li>Gemini</li><li>React Router</li><li>Leaflet</li></ul><div class="actions"><a class="btn btn--solid" href="https://arogyaplus-two.vercel.app" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open demo <svg class="icon" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M13.5 4h6.5v6.5M20 4l-9 9M17.5 14v5.5h-13v-13H10"/></svg><span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a><a class="btn" href="https://github.com/URAYUSHJAIN/Arogyaplus" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><svg class="icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M9 19.6c-4 1.2-4-2-5.6-2.4M14.6 21.6v-3.4c0-1 .1-1.4-.5-2 2.8-.3 5.6-1.4 5.6-6.2a4.8 4.8 0 0 0-1.3-3.3 4.5 4.5 0 0 0-.1-3.3s-1-.3-3.4 1.3a11.6 11.6 0 0 0-6 0C6.5 3.1 5.4 3.4 5.4 3.4a4.5 4.5 0 0 0-.1 3.3A4.8 4.8 0 0 0 4 10c0 4.8 2.8 5.9 5.6 6.2-.6.6-.6 1.2-.5 2v3.4"/></svg> View source on GitHub<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a></div><figure class="media"><video controls preload="none" playsinline src="/review-media/review/projects/arogya-plus/arogyaplus.mp4" aria-label="ArogyaPlus walkthrough video"></video><figcaption>Project walkthrough. No captions are available; the description below covers the same work.</figcaption></figure><h4>Problem</h4><p>Medical reports often contain terminology that is hard for a patient to interpret.</p><h4>What it does</h4><p>The application connects a report-upload flow to Gemini and presents explanations alongside hospital-map and emergency-summary interfaces. It explores how to make health information easier to navigate. Model output is informational and has not been clinically validated.</p><h4>Verified features</h4><ul class="bullets"><li>Report-upload and explanation interface</li><li>Hospital map</li><li>Emergency-summary interface</li></ul><h4>Architecture</h4><p>React/Vite interface → Gemini service integration; Leaflet/geolocation and browser storage support hospital-map and emergency-summary views.</p><p class="flag" data-review-mode>Needs owner review · media not yet cleared for release.</p></article><article class="record" id="record-calendar-dashboard" data-record-view="calendar-dashboard" hidden aria-labelledby="record-calendar-dashboard-title"><a class="back" href="/projects/" data-nav="projects"><svg class="icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M20.5 12h-16M10 6.5 4.5 12l5.5 5.5"/></svg> Projects</a><h3 class="record__title" id="record-calendar-dashboard-title" tabindex="-1">Calendar Dashboard</h3><p class="record__kicker">Full-stack · Experiment · Prototype</p><p class="lead">A calendar prototype with month views and persisted event creation, editing, and deletion.</p><ul class="tags" role="list" aria-label="Technologies"><li>HTML</li><li>JavaScript</li><li>Node.js</li><li>Express</li><li>PostgreSQL</li></ul><div class="actions"><a class="btn" href="https://github.com/URAYUSHJAIN/Calendar-Dashboard" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><svg class="icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M9 19.6c-4 1.2-4-2-5.6-2.4M14.6 21.6v-3.4c0-1 .1-1.4-.5-2 2.8-.3 5.6-1.4 5.6-6.2a4.8 4.8 0 0 0-1.3-3.3 4.5 4.5 0 0 0-.1-3.3s-1-.3-3.4 1.3a11.6 11.6 0 0 0-6 0C6.5 3.1 5.4 3.4 5.4 3.4a4.5 4.5 0 0 0-.1 3.3A4.8 4.8 0 0 0 4 10c0 4.8 2.8 5.9 5.6 6.2-.6.6-.6 1.2-.5 2v3.4"/></svg> View source on GitHub<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a></div><figure class="media"><video controls preload="none" playsinline src="/review-media/review/projects/calendar-dashboard/pwa-productivity.mp4" poster="/review-media/review/derived/calendar-dashboard/cal-modal.1280.webp" aria-label="Calendar Dashboard walkthrough video"></video><figcaption>Project walkthrough — part of a combined BuildLog episode; it starts at this project’s segment. No captions are available; the description below covers the same work.</figcaption></figure><ul class="gallery" role="list" aria-label="Screenshots"><li><a href="/review-media/review/derived/calendar-dashboard/cal-modal.1280.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="View larger image: Calendar Dashboard — interface capture (opens in a new tab)"><img src="/review-media/review/derived/calendar-dashboard/cal-modal.480.webp" width="480" height="480" alt="Calendar Dashboard — interface capture" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></a></li><li><a href="/review-media/review/derived/calendar-dashboard/cal-month.1280.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="View larger image: Calendar Dashboard — interface capture (opens in a new tab)"><img src="/review-media/review/derived/calendar-dashboard/cal-month.480.webp" width="480" height="480" alt="Calendar Dashboard — interface capture" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></a></li><li><a href="/review-media/review/derived/calendar-dashboard/cal-saved.1280.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="View larger image: Calendar Dashboard — interface capture (opens in a new tab)"><img src="/review-media/review/derived/calendar-dashboard/cal-saved.480.webp" width="480" height="480" alt="Calendar Dashboard — interface capture" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></a></li></ul><h4>Problem</h4><p>Calendar interactions need a consistent link between visual dates, event forms, and stored records.</p><h4>What it does</h4><p>A modular JavaScript frontend drives the main and mini calendars. An Express API stores event records in PostgreSQL. The README describes SQLite, but the server imports pg and the schema uses PostgreSQL features; this record follows the code.</p><h4>Verified features</h4><ul class="bullets"><li>Main and mini month calendars</li><li>Event create and edit dialogs</li><li>Event persistence API</li></ul><h4>Architecture</h4><p>Modular browser JavaScript → Express event API → PostgreSQL.</p><p class="flag" data-review-mode>Needs owner review · some details conflict between sources and are withheld · media not yet cleared for release.</p></article><article class="record" id="record-employee-management-system" data-record-view="employee-management-system" hidden aria-labelledby="record-employee-management-system-title"><a class="back" href="/projects/" data-nav="projects"><svg class="icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M20.5 12h-16M10 6.5 4.5 12l5.5 5.5"/></svg> Projects</a><h3 class="record__title" id="record-employee-management-system-title" tabindex="-1">Employee Management System</h3><p class="record__kicker">Full-stack · Experiment · Prototype</p><p class="lead">A Spring Boot REST API exercise for creating, searching, updating, and deleting employee records.</p><ul class="tags" role="list" aria-label="Technologies"><li>Java</li><li>Spring Boot</li><li>Spring Data JPA</li><li>H2</li><li>Maven</li><li>JUnit</li></ul><div class="actions"><a class="btn" href="https://github.com/URAYUSHJAIN/Employee-Management-System" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><svg class="icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M9 19.6c-4 1.2-4-2-5.6-2.4M14.6 21.6v-3.4c0-1 .1-1.4-.5-2 2.8-.3 5.6-1.4 5.6-6.2a4.8 4.8 0 0 0-1.3-3.3 4.5 4.5 0 0 0-.1-3.3s-1-.3-3.4 1.3a11.6 11.6 0 0 0-6 0C6.5 3.1 5.4 3.4 5.4 3.4a4.5 4.5 0 0 0-.1 3.3A4.8 4.8 0 0 0 4 10c0 4.8 2.8 5.9 5.6 6.2-.6.6-.6 1.2-.5 2v3.4"/></svg> View source on GitHub<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a></div><figure class="media"><video controls preload="none" playsinline src="/review-media/review/projects/aurevo/foundations.mp4" aria-label="Employee Management System walkthrough video"></video><figcaption>Project walkthrough — part of a combined BuildLog episode; it starts at this project’s segment. No captions are available; the description below covers the same work.</figcaption></figure><h4>Problem</h4><p>Managing structured records is a practical way to learn validation and REST API design.</p><h4>What it does</h4><p>The employee controller, service, and repository implement record operations through Spring Data JPA with an H2 database. The archive also contains separate student-management classes; these are not evidence of a larger deployed HR system.</p><h4>Verified features</h4><ul class="bullets"><li>Employee record operations</li><li>Search endpoints</li><li>Input validation</li></ul><h4>Architecture</h4><p>Spring controller → service → Spring Data JPA repository → H2 database.</p><p class="flag" data-review-mode>Needs owner review · media not yet cleared for release.</p></article><article class="record" id="record-inframe-studio" data-record-view="inframe-studio" hidden aria-labelledby="record-inframe-studio-title"><a class="back" href="/projects/" data-nav="projects"><svg class="icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M20.5 12h-16M10 6.5 4.5 12l5.5 5.5"/></svg> Projects</a><h3 class="record__title" id="record-inframe-studio-title" tabindex="-1">InFrame Studio</h3><p class="record__kicker">AI &amp; Data · Experiment · Prototype</p><p class="lead">A film-production decision-support prototype for scene risks, location checks, and alternative plans.</p><ul class="tags" role="list" aria-label="Technologies"><li>React</li><li>Vite</li><li>Node.js</li><li>Express</li><li>Azure OpenAI</li></ul><div class="actions"><a class="btn btn--solid" href="https://vord-guard.vercel.app/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open demo <svg class="icon" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M13.5 4h6.5v6.5M20 4l-9 9M17.5 14v5.5h-13v-13H10"/></svg><span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a><a class="btn" href="https://github.com/URAYUSHJAIN/VordGuard" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><svg class="icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M9 19.6c-4 1.2-4-2-5.6-2.4M14.6 21.6v-3.4c0-1 .1-1.4-.5-2 2.8-.3 5.6-1.4 5.6-6.2a4.8 4.8 0 0 0-1.3-3.3 4.5 4.5 0 0 0-.1-3.3s-1-.3-3.4 1.3a11.6 11.6 0 0 0-6 0C6.5 3.1 5.4 3.4 5.4 3.4a4.5 4.5 0 0 0-.1 3.3A4.8 4.8 0 0 0 4 10c0 4.8 2.8 5.9 5.6 6.2-.6.6-.6 1.2-.5 2v3.4"/></svg> View sour